<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pan-Asianism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as pan-Asian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pan-Asianism is an ideology advocating the unity of Asian peoples, often against Western colonialism. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> used pan-Asian rhetoric to justify its wartime expansion. Although cynically deployed, it contributed to the delegitimisation of European colonial rule in South-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The idea of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> united under Japanese leadership has been traced to the 16th century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> proposed to make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> one. The first lasting pan-Asianist body, the Shin'akai, was founded in Japan in 1877, followed in 1880 by the Kōakai, which taught Chinese and Korean and counted Chinese envoys and Korean reformers among its members. Before 1895 Japanese pan-Asianism treated China, Korea and Japan as equals. Japan's victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a> of 1904–1905 drew the interest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore">Rabindranath Tagore</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-aurobindo">Sri Aurobindo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a>, and the critic Okakura Kakuzō wrote of a love of the Ultimate and Universal shared by every Asian people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> the ideology turned towards nationalism. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">Secret societies</a> such as the Black Ocean Society and the Black Dragon Society used crime to advance Japanese expansion, and Asian lands were seen as stores of resources, as in the policies of Hakkō ichiu and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-east-asia-co-prosperity-sphere">Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere</a>. The historian Torsten Weber compares this to the Monroe Doctrine. Sun Yat-sen called for a pan-Asianism of the Rule of Right against the Western Rule of Might, while Li Dazhao saw Japan's version as an Asian Monroe Doctrine resting on aggression.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> some revolutionaries, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subhas-chandra-bose">Subhas Chandra Bose</a>, sought ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> against British rule. The Chinese historian Wang Ping divides the Japanese strand into a cooperative Classical Asianism until 1898, an expansive Greater Asianism until 1928 and the Co-Prosperity Sphere until 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> a newly independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s vision of Asian solidarity helped inspire the founding of the Asian Games in 1951, but Indian pan-Asianism faded after the India-China War of 1962. The later idea of Asian values has been seen as a revival of pan-Asianism, taken up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lee-kuan-yew">Lee Kuan Yew</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahathir-mohamad">Mahathir Mohamad</a> in the 1990s, while in India Ram Manohar Lohia hoped for a united socialist Asia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pan-Asianism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
